Most conclusions regarding the efficacy of explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) attribution methods were established on convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and have not been systematically re-validated on the diverse Vision Transformer (ViT) architectures that now dominate computer vision. This paper presents a controlled benchmark that measures attribution quality along five complementary axes: faithfulness, localization, robustness, complexity, and computational cost. Using a standardized evaluation suite, we evaluate 13 attribution methods from four algorithmic families on matched-scale backbones spanning CNNs, isotropic ViTs, and hierarchical, hybrid, and linear-attention transformers. Our findings reveal that the attribution rankings do not transfer between architectures. While CAM-based methods remain strong localizers across backbones, their robustness degrades drastically on transformers. In contrast, attention-native methods exhibit superior stability but poor spatial localization. Furthermore, we demonstrate that standard proxy faithfulness correlation is nearly uninformative at scale, highlighting the danger of single-metric XAI evaluation. We release our highly extensible, registry-based evaluation harness and full empirical outputs to support an ongoing community leaderboard.
